{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788192922,"nonce":"pA3KBnsOvIf6x/DMgofD5jzx9a4=","title":"Post-raid procedure: an event-triggered checklist, and the downed-hostiles read it depends on","message":"Evan, 2026-08-31. The first concrete instance of a general idea: take\ndeterministic decisions off the agent by running a fixed procedure when a\ntrigger fires, and be intentional about what is NOT deterministic enough to\nautomate.\n\nTHE TRIGGER IS ALREADY COMPUTABLE, AND ALREADY CORRECT\n\n`DigestVerb.ThreatSection` counts hostiles with\n`if (p.Downed || p.Dead || !p.HostileTo(Faction.OfPlayer)) continue;`\n\nSo `threats.hostiles == 0` already means \"no hostile is currently STANDING\".\nEvan's framing, which is the right one: that IS deterministic — the value can\nchange at any moment (a downed raider wakes and it flips back to 1), but a live\nvalue is not non-determinism. No new predicate is needed for \"the fight is over\".\n\nTHE PROCEDURE\n\n1. **Rescue downed colonists** with the nearest capable pawn. First because it\n   is the only time-critical step — they bleed out.\n2. **Finish off downed enemies.** Allow Tool's \"finish off\" would be the clean\n   route; **Allow Tool is NOT on the agent bench** (checked), so the fallback is\n   the only path: a drafted `attack` order. Evan's call, and it is correct —\n   seek-at-will will NOT do this, because `SeekRegistry.ShouldSeek` targets\n   threats and a downed pawn is not one.\n3. **Re-engagement needs no step.** If a raider stands up mid-procedure,\n   `threats.hostiles` flips and the seek-at-will pawns engage on their own.\n   That is the part of this that is already automated and should stay that way.\n\nWHAT THIS DEPENDS ON — THE MISSING READ\n\n**Nothing currently surfaces downed enemies.** `threats` deliberately skips\nthem, and while `pawns {filter:\"hostile\"}` finds them, no observer answers \"here\nare the bodies on the ground that might get up\". The procedure cannot be written\nwithout it. That read is cheap and should land first: downed hostiles, where\nthey are, and whether they are still bleeding out on their own.\n\nTHE POLICY THAT MUST STAY VISIBLE\n\nKilling downed raiders is a POLICY, not a mechanical step. It forfeits prisoners\n(recruits, ransom, organs) and some ideology precepts punish it. It is the right\ndefault *today* only because there is no prison — see the prisoner issue filed\nalongside this. So the finish-off step must be a flag the procedure reads, not a\nhardcoded action, or turning it off later becomes a rewrite instead of a config\nchange.\n\nTHE OPEN QUESTION THIS DOES NOT SETTLE — where a procedure LIVES\n\nIn the mod as C#: zero token cost, but invisible to the agent and unreasonable\nabout. In the playbook as markdown (4.1 already specifies `checklists/morning.md`\nand a `trigger-context` field on every lesson): reviewable in a diff, inherited\nby every session, but the agent pays to read and follow it every time. This is\nthe same cost question as the attribution work and should be answered once, for\nboth, rather than twice.\n\nNote 4.1 already anticipates trigger-keyed procedures — it just scoped the idea\nto one trigger (morning) and never generalised it. This issue is the second\ntrigger, and the reason to generalise.\n\nAcceptance\n\n- A read that surfaces downed hostiles: id, position, whether stabilised.\n- The procedure runs on `threats.hostiles` reaching 0 and is re-entrant — if the\n  count flips back up mid-procedure it yields, and resumes after.\n- Rescue precedes finish-off, and the reason is stated where a reader will see it.\n- Finish-off is a flag, defaulting on only while no prison exists.\n- Demonstrated on the bench: a raid fought, colonists rescued, downed enemies\n  finished, and a raider that wakes mid-procedure engaged by seek pawns with NO\n  new agent decision.","files":null}]}